(
    [action] => save_process
    [uuid] => 47bb0aac-841f-4fd5-886f-c4723d9e6da8
    [isdraft] => 1
    [process_name] => Environmental impact of Oaxaca cheese production and wastewater from artisanal dairies under two scenarios in Aculco, State of Mexico
    [category] => Manufacturing/311 Food Manufacturing/31151 Cheese Manufacturing
    [general_comment] => The environmental impacts in the artisan cheese sector in the central region of Mexico provide a particular panorama of the environmental situation of this industry. The objective of this study was to evaluate environmental impacts through a life cycle analysis in 10 impact categories: global warming, stratospheric ozone depletion, fine particle formation, ozone formation, terrestrial ecosystems, terrestrial acidification, eutrophication of fresh water, terrestrial ecotoxicity, human ecotoxicity. non-carcinogenic toxicity, scarcity of fossil resources and water consumption. And the calculation of the carbon footprint with the IPCC, 2013 of 1 kg of Oaxaca cheese in four dairies artisanal in the State of Mexico. The main hotspots were whey, transportation, and electricity. The reduction of the impact generated by wastewater can be associated with the implementation and improvement of eco-innovations such as the use of biodigesters, however their use generates impacts on SOD, TA, FA, TEc and FRs due to the composition of the whey residue. The renewal of refrigeration equipment is another alternative to reduce electricity consumption, which on average consumes 0.04 kWh per one kg of Oaxaca cheese in cheese factories that have new refrigerators, and 0.14 kWh when refrigerators are reused, mainly impacting the FRs. The average carbon footprint of Oaxaca cheese was 15.6 CO 2 kg eq, which allows us to have a measure of the behavior of this food. A sensitivity analysis was carried out with different scenarios with wastewater where it was found that the handling of whey is essential for reducing impacts.
    [technology_description] => 
    [geo_description] => 
    [process_type] => 
    [time_desc] => 
    [version] => 01.00.000
    [valid_from] => 
    [valid_until] => 
    [project] => This document was supported by the National Council of Science and Technology (CONACYT) for the project "Estudio de productos lácteos en el norte del estado de México a través de un S-ACV: Estudio de caso de dos tipos de quesos" 4285/2017/CI (Universidad Autónoma del Estado de México) and "Repertorios y estrategias culturales de organizaciones y actores en la sustentabilidad de la cadena agroalimentaria de productos lácteos PAPIIT-IN305620 (National Autonomous University of Mexico).
    [lci_method] => The LCA was carried out in accordance with ISO 14040. It consisted of four phases: the establishment of the scope and objectives, the elaboration of the environmental inventories, and the generation and interpretation of the results.
    [ds_data_selection] => The impact of milk prior to arriving at the dairy was eliminated from the calculation in order to focus on the critical points within the cheese production industry.
    [ds_data_treatment] => The data for the inventories were obtained through direct observation and eighteen interviews. Information was corroborated with a veterinary doctor. The data are reflective of a one-year production cycle: Monthly production data were first calculated. Then, the annual averages were estimated for a functional unit of 1 kg of cheese. The values presented are the average of the four dairies studied.
    [data_collection_period] => June 2018 to June 2019
    [ds_data_completeness] => The inventories were calculated for each of the four dairies using data specific to Oaxaca cheese production. Monthly production data were first calculated, then annual averages were estimated for the functional unit.
    [completeness_text] => The study provides a gate-to-gate inventory for the cheese production process, excluding the upstream impacts of raw milk production.
    [sources_text] => Aguirre-Villegas, H.A., Kraatz, S., Milani, F., Newenhouse, A., Passos-Fonseca, T., Reinemann, D., 2011. Understand the Footprint Carbon Cheese. Madison. Aguirre-Villegas, H.A., Milani, F.X., Kraatz, S., Reinemann, D.J., 2012. Life cycle impact assessment and allocations methods development for cheese and whey processing. Am. Soc. Agric. Biol. Eng. 55, 613–627. Alves, E.C., Soares, B.B., de Almeida Neto, J.A., Rodrigues, L.B., 2019. Strategies for reducing the environmental impacts of organic mozzarella cheese production. J. Clean. Prod. 223, 226–237. Antwi, V., 2010. Use of Monte Carlo Analysis in Life Cycle Assessment: Case of Study- Fruits Processing Plant in Ghana. Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology Kumasi-Ghana. AOAC, 1990. Official Methods of Analysis, 15th. Association of Oficial Analytical Chemists, Gaithersburg, MD. PRé, various authors, 2016. SimaPro Database Manual Methods Library. PRé sustainability, Netherlands. Bacenetti, J., Bava, L., Schievano, A., Zucali, M., 2018. Whey protein concentrate (WPC) production: environmental impact assessment. J. Food Eng. Bachmann, R.T., Becerra, M., Cerdn, E., Gonzlez-Siso, I., Hernandez, J.E., Johnson, A.C., 2008. Biogas production from cheese whey: past, present and future. In: Becerra, Manuel, Cerdán, M.E., González-Siso, I. (Eds.), Advances in Cheese Whey Utilization. Transworld research network, Kerala, India, pp. 35–80. Bava, L., Bacenetti, J., Gislon, G., Pellegrino, L., D'Incecco, P., Sandrucci, A., Tamburini, A., Fiala, M., Zucali, M., 2018. Impact assessment of traditional food manufacturing: the case of Grana Padano cheese. Sci. Total Environ. 626, 1200–1209. Berlin, J., 2002. Environmental life cycle assessment (LCA) of Swedish semi-hard cheese. Int. Dairy J. 12, 939–953. Bourlakis, M., Maglaras, G., Gallear, D., Fotopoulos, C., 2014. Examining sustainability performance in the supply chain: the case of the Greek dairy sector. Ind. Market. Manag. 43, 56–66. Canellada, 