    [uuid] => e5561a0f-fe1c-4ef0-84d8-511dd210541c
    [process_name] => Life cycle assessment of nine representative agroindustrial systems of sugar production in Mexico
    [category] => Manufacturing/311 Food Manufacturing/3113 Sugar and Confectionery Product Manufacturing
    [general_comment] => Mexico is the sixth-largest producer of sucrose worldwide. Therefore, evaluating the environmental impacts of Mexican sugar and proposing impact mitigation strategies is of high importance since this would support better environmental performance and foster competition within the sector. However, the technological variability that occurs between farms and sugar mills, together with the different types of sugar that are produced, makes it difficult to identify a single agro-industrial production system for sugar production, that represents the entire sector. The aim of this study is to develop a life cycle assessment study of nine representative sugar production systems in Mexico, considering 1 ton of sugar as a functional unit while also considering the types of sugar and technological level. The results showed that conventional systems demonstrated a better environmental performance for all the impact categories analyzed, primarily because they have a lower energy requirement in cultivation and irrigation, require less fertilizers and applied pesticides, and in the industrial stage, require less chemicals for clarification. Regarding the types of sugar, Muscovado presented the lowest environmental impacts in all the categories analyzed. The results would suggest that the strategic avenues for further improving the environmental profile of sugarcane production and processing could include (a) continued pursuit of high yields, (b) precision application of fertilizers and (c) maximizing the utilization of coproducts.
    [technology_description] => 
    [geo_description] => 
    [process_type] => 
    [time_desc] => 
    [version] => 01.00.000
    [valid_from] => 2021
    [valid_until] => 
    [project] => 
    [lci_method] => ReCiPe midpoint (H) V1.12
    [ds_data_selection] => The study is based on data from 18 sugar mills in Veracruz state. Systems were classified based on a hierarchical cluster analysis of agronomic management and agro-industrial yields. Capital goods were excluded for simplicity due to their long lifespan. Only national distribution was considered for muscovado sugar, while national and international distribution was considered for standard and refined sugar.
    [ds_data_treatment] => A hierarchical cluster analysis was performed using IBM SPSS Statistics 20 to classify the systems into three technological levels (conventional, semimechanized, mechanized) based on agronomic management and yields.
    [data_collection_period] => 2016/2017 harvest season.
    [ds_data_completeness] => The study covers the main life cycle stages: agricultural production, sugar milling, commercialization, distillery, and fodder production. It is based on data from Veracruz, which is representative of the Mexican national sugar sector (42% of cultivated area). Nine representative systems were modeled to account for technological variability.
    [completeness_text] => The study is comprehensive for its defined scope, covering the main life cycle stages and considering different technological levels and product types. However, it excludes capital goods and cogeneration, which are noted as limitations.
    [sources_text] => Array
    [modeling_constants] => Functional Unit: 1 ton of cane sugar. The production of farm-used capital goods such as tractors and harvesters was not included. Cogeneration was not considered due to its limited use. For cattle feed, it was assumed that corn was displaced with silage production, and molasses had an 85% efficiency performance in relation to corn.
    [access_restrictions] => 
