    [process_name] => Environmental Impacts Associated with Intensive Production in Pig Farms in Mexico through Life Cycle Assessment
    [category] => Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing and Hunting/112 Animal Production and Aquaculture/1122 Hog and Pig Farming
    [general_comment] => In this research, environmental impacts associated with the intensive production of pigs on a farm in Mexico were determined through the application of life cycle assessment methodology. The research was focused on the following stages of the product system: (i) pig rearing and growth phases; (ii) production operations in the pig-house; (iii) the supply of feed. The life cycle inventory database was mainly made up of data collected in field visits to local farms. The functional unit was defined as one finished swine weighing 124 kg. The results for the selected impact categories of carbon, water, and energy footprints were 538.62 kg CO2eq, 21.34 m³, and 1773.79 MJ, respectively. The greatest impact was generated in the final stages of pig fattening, mainly due to the large quantity of feed supplied. The impacts caused by operation of the pig farm were less significant, their contribution in all cases was less than a third of the total quantified values. The energy conversion of pig slurry improves the environmental performance of the product system by reducing the carbon footprint.
    [technology_description] => 
    [geo_description] => 
    [process_type] => 
    [time_desc] => 
    [version] => 01.00.000
    [valid_from] => 2021
    [valid_until] => 
    [project] => 
    [lci_method] => The methodology was based on ISO 14040: 2006 and ISO 14044: 2006, including definition of objectives and scope, life cycle inventory (LCI), evaluation of life cycle impact, and life cycle interpretation.
    [ds_data_selection] => Capital goods such as facility construction were excluded. The use of machinery for land preparation and supply transportation were included.
    [ds_data_treatment] => Material and energy balances were carried out to determine all the flows. Mass allocation was applied according to the co-products generated in the product system.
    [data_collection_period] => 
    [ds_data_completeness] => The study focused on three main stages: (i) pig rearing and growth phases (Reproduction, Gestation, Piglet Breeding, Fattening); (ii) production operations in the pig-house; (iii) the supply of feed. Capital goods were excluded.
    [completeness_text] => High for the defined 'cradle-to-gate' scope, covering feed production, transport, and on-farm activities. The exclusion of facility construction is a noted limitation.
    [sources_text] => Array
    [modeling_constants] => Capital goods, such as the construction of facilities, were excluded as their contributions do not represent significant results and are considerably amortized. The use of machinery for land preparation and supply transportation were included. For enteric gases, the Tier-1 method was used with methane emission factors reported in the literature.
