    [process_name] => Environmental Impacts Associated with the Production and Packing of Persian Lemon in Mexico through Life-Cycle Assessment
    [category] => A:Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing and Hunting/111:Crop Production/1113:Fruit and Tree Nut Farming
    [general_comment] => In this study, the environmental impacts associated with the intensive production of Persian lemons are assessed, including the agricultural and packing phases of the fresh fruit. A life-cycle assessment (LCA) tool was used in accordance with the ISO 14040 and 14044 standards and implemented in SimaPro PhD (9.2) software. The life-cycle inventory database was primarily composed of data collected during field visits to local lemon orchards and the main packing company in the region. The functional unit was defined as 1 kg of packed fresh Persian lemons. The selected impact categories were the carbon footprint, water footprint, and energy footprint, and the results obtained for the defined functional unit were 405.8 g CO2 eq, 40.3 L of water, and 5.9 MJ, respectively. The industrial packing phase of the fruits had a greater impact on the carbon and energy footprints, mostly due to the manufacturing of packaging materials and cardboard boxes, followed by the transportation of supplies. Regarding the water footprint, the agricultural phase was identified as the most significant contributor to water consumption, primarily attributed to maintenance operations and the application of agrochemicals.
    [technology_description] => 
    [geo_description] => 
    [process_type] => 
    [time_desc] => 
    [version] => 01.00.000
    [valid_from] => 2024
    [valid_until] => 
    [project] => 
    [lci_method] => The methodology was based on the guidelines of the ISO14040:2006 and ISO14044:2006 standards, including the phases of definition of goal and scope, life-cycle inventory analysis (LCI), life-cycle impact assessment (LCIA), and interpretation of results.
    [ds_data_selection] => The study focused on intensive Persian lemon production. Capital goods such as facilities and infrastructure were excluded from the system boundaries, although agricultural implements were included. Transportation was considered for supplying inputs and for moving the product between production phases.
    [ds_data_treatment] => Collected data were processed with material and energy balances to verify their suitability and then quantified with respect to the functional unit. In accordance with ISO 14044, load allocations by mass were applied for the distribution of environmental impacts generated in the products and co-products.
    [data_collection_period] => The article was submitted in December 2023 and published in May 2024. The field visits were conducted over a three-month period prior to submission.
    [ds_data_completeness] => The study covers the agricultural and industrial packing phases. Data for inputs such as agrochemicals, energy, water, transportation, and packaging materials were collected and validated through material and energy balances. Key inputs' manufacturing processes were considered.
    [completeness_text] => The study demonstrates a high level of completeness for the defined cradle-to-gate scope, with extensive primary data collection from key actors in the supply chain and validation through mass and energy balances.
    [sources_text] => Array
    [modeling_constants] => The functional unit was 1 kg of packaged Persian lemon. The scope was “from cradle to gate”, considering cultivation, harvesting, and packaging. The supply of required inputs and their transport were included. Agricultural implements were considered, while facilities and infrastructure were not. Load allocations by mass were applied for the distribution of environmental impacts.
    [access_restrictions] => 0
