    [action] => save_process
    [uuid] => f3d08c25-67c9-49c3-a16f-be9d2a8660f3
    [isdraft] => 1
    [process_name] => The water, energy, and land footprint of tilapia aquaculture in mexico, a comparison of the footprints of fish and meat
    [category] => Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing and Hunting/112 Animal Production and Aquaculture/1125 Aquaculture
    [general_comment] => In the food-energy-water (FEW) nexus, livestock has a dominant place. It is generally considered as water, energy and land-intensive. Aquaculture could provide additional animal protein and contribute to meeting the food demand. However, aquaculture requires natural resources and causes freshwater pollution due to aquafeed, fertilizer, and hormone use. This study assesses the sustainability of aquaculture using the indicators water footprint (WF), energy footprint (EF) and land footprint (LF), comparing results with livestock. It uses extensive, semi-intensive and intensive Tilapia aquaculture in Mexico as a case study including broodstock, breeding, fattening, processing, and transportation phases. Tilapia production in intensive aquaculture has the largest footprints. The functional unit is one ton of Tilapia fillet. The system boundary covers the production chain from eggs to fillet, excluding packaging, retailing, transportation, and cooking by consumers. Results show that from a FEW nexus perspective, it is not more sustainable to replace livestock with Tilapia, as it requires more freshwater and pollutes larger amounts of water, while energy and land footprints are comparable.
    [technology_description] => 
    [geo_description] => 
    [process_type] => 
    [time_desc] => 
    [version] => 01.00.000
    [valid_from] => 
    [valid_until] => 
    [project] => 
    [lci_method] => Chain analysis approach for assessing Water Footprint (WF), Energy Footprint (EF), and Land Footprint (LF). The WF calculation follows the methodology from the Water Footprint Assessment Manual (Hoekstra et al., 2011).
    [ds_data_selection] => The study focuses on three specific aquaculture systems (extensive, semi-intensive, intensive) in Mexico. It excludes wastewater treatment as most farms reportedly do not treat their wastewater. The comparison is made with industrial systems for beef, poultry, and pork.
    [ds_data_treatment] => A chain analysis approach with seven calculation steps was used: 1. Estimation of direct blue WF; 2. Calculation of direct LF; 3. Calculation of aquafeed offered; 4. Allocation of aquafeed; 5. Estimation of the WF from aquafeed; 6. Calculation of EF; and finally, 7. Calculation of the direct grey WF.
    [data_collection_period] => The article was published in 2020, using data from various sources published between 1986 and 2019.
    [ds_data_completeness] => The study covers all major production stages from egg to fillet. Inputs like aquafeed, fertilizer, hormones, energy for pumping/aeration, and transportation are considered. However, packaging and retail stages are excluded.
    [completeness_text] => The study is comprehensive for the defined scope of cradle-to-processing-gate for Tilapia fillet production.
    [sources_text] => Aina, Q., Agustina, S.E., 2015. Energy consumption of pond-based intensive Tilapia aquaculture system. Case study in Wunut, Central Java, Indonesia. In: The 22nd Tri-U International Joint Seminar and Symposium. Jiangsu University. China.; Baltazar, P.M., 2007. La Tilapia en el Perú: acuicultura, mercado, y perspectivas. Revista peruana de Biología 13 (3), 267-273.; Bazilian, M., et al., 2011. Considering the energy, water and food nexus: towards an integrated modelling approach. Energy Policy 39, 7896-7906.; Bhujel, R.C., 2013. On-farm feed management practices for Nile Tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) in Thailand. In: Hasan, M.R., New, M.B (Eds.), On-farm Feeding and Feed Management in Aquaculture. FAO, Rome, pp. 159-189.; Borucke, M., et al., 2013. Accounting for demand and supply of the biosphere's regenerative capacity: the national footprint accounts' underlying methodology and framework. Ecol Indic 24, 518-533.; Boyd, C.E., et al., 2007. Indicators of resource use efficiency and environmental performance in fish and crustacean aquaculture. Reviews in Fisheries science 15 (4), 327-360.; De Vries, M., De Boer, I.J.M., 2010. Comparing environmental impacts for livestock products: a review of life cycle assessments. Livest Sci 128 (1-3), 1-11.; FAO, 2003, 2005, 2017.; Flores-Nava, A., 2007. Feeds and fertilizers for sustainable aquaculture development: a regional review for Latin America. FAO Fisheries Technical Paper. No. 497.; Gerbens-Leenes, P.W., et al., 2013. The water footprint of poultry, pork and beef: a comparative study in different countries and production systems. Water Resources and Industry 1, 25-36.; Hoekstra, A.Y., et al., 2011. The Water Footprint Assessment Manual: Setting the Global Standard. Earthscan, London, UK.; Kramer, K.J., Moll, 1995. Energie voedt, Nadere Analyses Van Het Directe Energieverbruik Van voedingsmiddelen. University of Groningen, Groningen, The Netherlands.; Mekonnen, M.M., Hoekstra, A.Y., 2010b. The green, Blue and Grey Water Footprint of Farm Animals and Animal Products. UNESCO-IHE, Delft, the Netherlands.; SAGARPA, 2013, 2015.; Williams, A., et al., 2006. Determining the Environmental Burdens and Resource Use in the Production of Agricultural and Horticultural Commodities. Main Report. Defra Research Project ISO205.
    [modeling_constants] => Functional Unit: one ton of Tilapia fillet. System boundaries include broodstock, breeding, fattening, processing, and transportation phases; they exclude packaging, retailing, and consumer transportation and cooking. Fillet yield is assumed to be 33%. One production cycle per year. For intensive systems, an average water refreshment rate of 250% per day is used. An average transport distance of 100 km is assumed. All crops for aquafeed are assumed to be grown in Mexico.
